# A New Life
Tamil title: புதுவாழ்வு

"He refuses even biscuits. He will not eat chocolates either. He does not want almonds. If I offer milk, coffee or Ovaltine, he only cries. The child is wasting away to a twig. I do not know what to do," Kantha tells her servant Velammal about her child's lack of appetite and asks her to go to the doctor for medicine.

The baby screams without pause. It has been wretched since morning; one cannot bear to hear it. Gruel or even water would quiet the crying. But what is here? What can I give it? There is no milk. Even if I go to the neighbours, bare my teeth and plead, no one gives anything. This wretched child will not stop either. What can I possibly do? God, is this too one of your trials?" Karuppi, wife of the handcart puller Kandan, says anxiously after striking the child.

"Just two minutes, brother! One stroke and I made fourteen hundred rupees. I bought that share at 1,600. At eight this morning a 'Bombay sparrow' whispered a secret: the share price would fall after nine. My heart raced. I looked around and spotted that Sonachala Chettiar in the share market. 'Absolute nonsense,' I told him, and rattled on without pause. The Chettiar gave way. He developed a craving for our share. I closed at 3,000 rupees. The share went to him and the cheque came to us. The Chettiar will discover his fate this evening; it will not fetch even a thousand rupees." Thus Chidambaram, a share-market trader, tells his friend of his cleverness.

"I went at six. All I had was an appam and some water. I laboured until noon. I swallowed two mouthfuls of the rice Valli brought and lay down for a while. The moment I heard the foreman's voice, I got up and worked until six in the evening. He paid me only these four annas, and I have just found that one anna among them is counterfeit. What am I to do?" Kumarappan, the labourer, tells his angry wife, trying to pacify her.

"Such fun! Such delight! The cocktail party was splendid. When whisky, brandy, wine, gin and all the rest went down together, I became rather peculiar. I am accustomed to cocktails, of course, but yesterday was a little excessive. I took the car and set off. The road seemed to twist and wind. The driver said he would drive. 'Damn it,' I told him. A car came towards us and we nearly collided. Its driver slammed on the brakes; the oncoming car stopped too. DSP Durasami got out. He saw my condition, laughed, and took me away with him. Our driver brought our motorcar home. I created quite a commotion at the DSP's house as well. I did not recover until dawn. I apologised to the DSP. 'This is ordinary, brother,' he said, 'but you must not drive at such a time. Had you told that fellow to drive and gone peacefully to sleep, there would have been no trouble.' The DSP is right; that is what I must do from now on." The morning after his drunken revel, minor mirasdar Marthandam recounts it to his friend Narayanasami.

"What can I say, Ekatha? Only now do I understand. Those policemen certainly beat me hard. I did not drink very much; I had only my usual amount. Yesterday there was a new clerk in the toddy shop. He did not mix in much water, so it was rather strong. I came outside and met my brother-in-law, who had also been drinking. We were merely talking when a quarrel broke out. He hit me and I hit him. He shouted, I shouted, and a crowd gathered. The police came. He slipped away, but they caught me, locked me in the station, and beat me. They certainly struck hard. I cannot even go to work today; my whole body aches. I swear to you, dear, I will never drink again. I swear it upon Mariamman." Thus Virappan, a woodcutter, tells his wife.

"He does not have even one hour's rest. It is work from morning to night. In the morning he goes 'swimming.' Then comes cards. As soon as he returns and eats, the car arrives and he goes to the office. He looks at the post, signs cheques, and scarcely begins reading the paper before club members call on the telephone and ask him to come play tennis in the evening. When that is over, he comes home and eats. Before he finishes dinner, someone else arrives and says they must go to a picture. He returns at midnight and falls asleep exhausted. Is there time to talk? Is there a moment's peace at home? He is always busy outside. Car after car arrives. And fifty people work in his office! What more must one amass by labouring like this? Does he lack money or good fortune? What he already has would feed him for a lifetime. Yet he wanders about in this fashion!" Rose Bai tells a friend about her husband Manohar.

"Of course he stays at home. Where else can he go? He could go only if there were work. Is there a shop, company, office, or any employment for him? He eats, chews betel, plays cards on the outer veranda, and comes inside to play dice. In the evening he goes out for an hour. He returns saying, 'I saw this man and that man; they said something might be available next month; they said no post was vacant.' Then it is cards as before. Why should he leave the house when there is nothing outside? I do not know on what day he will find work or when my family will prosper." Vedam, distressed that her husband cannot find employment, speaks to her neighbour Parvathi.

"Would green and red look beautiful? Would blue and red match? Would stripes be good, or would a decorative design look better? Shall we place a statue of Krishna or one of Rama? Would dolls look beautiful at both ends of the parapet, or would flowerpots look better?" Usurer Varadappa walks about the third floor of his new house asking the ornamental builder these questions.

"Last night's rain brought down the back wall. I propped it with a casuarina pole so it would not lean further, but the pole could not bear the weight and began to break. Frightened, I tried to straighten it, and by then the whole wall collapsed. My leg was badly hurt. With the wall down, drain water has entered the house. Everything is mud; it is unbearable." Dharman, who lives in a thatched house, describes the collapse of his home and asks for a loan at the Marwari's shop.

The names here are Kandan, Velan, Dharman and Rose Bai. In Western countries the names might be Tom, Dick and Lucy. Yet scenes like these, with some rolling in wealth and others struggling in poverty, exist everywhere, even in America, celebrated as the land of millionaires.

Every country has two distinct classes: those without property and those who possess it. The class division, and the hatred, fury and unrest it produces, exist everywhere, today as well as long ago.

There is only one place where this terrible division does not exist. That place is Soviet Russia. What further evidence is needed to call it a good country, to praise, commend and salute it?

To attain that condition, the blood shed and courage displayed by its people on 7 November 1917 astonished the world. The sacred lamp born from their sacrifice is that new world.

There is no one who asks what the November Revolution accomplished. If anyone does, we may strike our chest and declare: in a great country, that revolution gave full life, new life and a good life to people who until the previous day had lived only half a life.

17 November 1946

---NOTES---
The source prints “1977 நவம்பர் 7” for the Russian Revolution. Its reference to the November Revolution, the article's own 1946 date, and the established historical date resolve this as 7 November 1917; the Tamil source remains verbatim. Colloquial dialogue and period English loanwords are retained in an equivalent conversational register. “ஈயம்” in the wage passage denotes a false or base-metal coin, rendered “counterfeit.”
